READ THE PATH BEFORE IMPLEMENTING LEVER 1, AND MOST OF IT IS ALREADY THERE.

REACHABILITY, mapped precisely because a plausible misreading points the other way:
  crossing_minimization -> crossing_minimization_impl::<true,true,true,true> (lib.rs:12293)
  barycenter -> barycenter_crossing_count
  if crossings == 0 -> e-graph SKIPPED entirely (lib.rs:12419)
  else apply_egraph_ordering_pass (lib.rs:12422) -> egraph_optimized_order_for_rank (13933)

So the e-graph IS on the production path, via `egraph_ordering`. I nearly reported the opposite: `egraph_crossing::extract_best_ordering` has ZERO callers anywhere including the bench, and grepping for that first suggests the whole e-graph is dead. It is not - there are TWO modules and the live one is egraph_ordering. Anyone re-deriving this should check line 12422 before concluding otherwise.

LEVER 1 ("expected-loss gate for e-graph vs greedy based on density/layer width/budget, with deterministic fallback") IS SUBSTANTIALLY IMPLEMENTED at lib.rs:13939:

    if current_order.len() < 2 || !should_use_egraph(current_order.len()) { return None; }

  - the `< 2` guard is LOSS-FREE by construction: a rank with one node has exactly one possible order, so skipping cannot change output;
  - should_use_egraph is the WIDTH gate (egraph_ordering.rs:673, currently <= 100 nodes/layer);
  - the zero-crossings early exit at 12419 is the cheapest gate of all;
  - estimate_egraph_size is already computed per rank at 14007, so a BUDGET-aware gate has its input available.

WHAT REMAINS is the density- and budget-aware variant, and I did NOT land it. It trades quality for speed, this bead explicitly forbids keeping such a trade unmeasured, and the freeze forbids measuring. Landing a speculative skip here is the shape that already cost this project once - a guardrail forced large mindmaps onto the O(N^2) path, and a dedicated algorithm priced above its fallback is a red flag rather than a win.

LANDED INSTEAD: crates/fm-layout/tests/egraph_gate_is_loss_free.rs, pinning the width gate from BOTH sides. A width gate is exactly where a quality regression hides quietly - lower it far enough and the e-graph stops running on the diagrams it exists for, nothing fails, no error appears, and the only symptom is more crossings in output nobody diffs numerically. The tests assert ordinary widths (2-40) are ADMITTED, pathological widths (1k-100k) are REFUSED, and the gate is MONOTONE - a non-monotone gate is the shape a density heuristic can accidentally produce, admitting 50 and 200 while refusing 100. It deliberately does not assert the constant is 100, so the threshold stays free to move on evidence.

NEXT MEASURED SESSION: the site is lib.rs:12419-12429, the per-rank decision is 13939, and the budget input is already computed at 14007. Levers 2-4 (branchless inversion count, preallocated scratch, SIMD bitset adjacency) touch the live path and do NOT trade quality, so they are the safer ones to measure first.

description:
# Sequence Diagram IR Metadata

## Purpose
All advanced sequence diagram features (activation, notes, fragments, autonumber, participant groups) need structured metadata that doesn't fit in the generic node/edge model. This task adds a sequence-specific metadata structure to the IR.

## Types to Add
1. IrSequenceMeta struct containing:
   - autonumber: bool
   - activations: Vec<IrActivation> { participant: IrNodeId, start_edge: usize, end_edge: usize, depth: usize }
   - notes: Vec<IrSequenceNote> { position: NotePosition, participants: Vec<IrNodeId>, text: String }
   - fragments: Vec<IrSequenceFragment> { kind: FragmentKind, label: String, start_edge: usize, end_edge: usize, alternatives: Vec<FragmentAlternative>, children: Vec<usize> }
   - participant_groups: Vec<IrParticipantGroup> { label: String, color: Option<String>, participants: Vec<IrNodeId> }
   - lifecycle_events: Vec<IrLifecycleEvent> { kind: Create|Destroy, participant: IrNodeId, at_edge: usize }
2. NotePosition enum: LeftOf, RightOf, Over
3. FragmentKind enum: Loop, Alt, Opt, Par, Critical, Break, Rect
4. Add sequence_meta: Option<IrSequenceMeta> to MermaidDiagramIr

## This unblocks: ALL sequence diagram advanced feature tasks

